# MZ Logistics Customer Platform - Flutter Rules

Follow the global MZ Logistics rules.

This application serves MZ Logistics customers on:

- Web
- Android
- iOS

The same codebase must provide an excellent experience on both mobile and web.

## Architecture

Use a clean feature-based architecture.

Recommended structure:

lib/
  core/
  shared/
  features/
    auth/
    home/
    shipments/
    quotations/
    jobs/
    trips/
    tracking/
    payments/
    invoices/
    profile/
    notifications/

Each feature should separate:

- Presentation
- State management
- Domain/business logic where needed
- Data/API layer

Do not put API logic directly inside widgets.

## UI

Customer experience should feel simple and premium.

The customer should easily understand:

- How to create shipment request.
- Shipment status.
- Received quotations.
- Quotation comparison.
- Payment status.
- Job progress.
- Trip progress.
- Tracking.
- Invoices.

Do not expose unnecessary technical complexity.

## Web vs Mobile

Do not use the same layout blindly.

Desktop/web:
- Side navigation when appropriate.
- Wider forms.
- Data tables.
- Multi-column layouts.
- Quotation comparison layouts.
- Filters and search.

Mobile:
- Bottom navigation or compact navigation.
- Cards where appropriate.
- Step-by-step forms.
- Mobile-friendly quotation comparison.
- Large touch targets.

## Responsive

Use responsive/adaptive layouts.

Avoid fixed widths and heights unless necessary.

Never allow:
- Overflow.
- Cut text.
- Broken RTL.
- Desktop-only interactions.

## State Management

Use one consistent state-management approach across the project.

Do not mix multiple state-management libraries without strong reason.

Business state must not live directly in UI widgets.

## Navigation

Centralize routes.

Protect authenticated routes.

Support deep linking where practical for Flutter Web.

## Localization

All text must use localization resources.

Support:
- Arabic RTL
- English LTR

No hardcoded user-facing strings.

## API

Use a centralized API client.

Handle:
- Authentication tokens.
- Timeouts.
- Server errors.
- Validation errors.
- Unauthorized responses.
- Connection errors.

Use typed models.

## User Experience

All actions must provide proper feedback.

Examples:
- Creating shipment.
- Submitting request.
- Selecting quotation.
- Payment.
- Uploading documents.

Use confirmation dialogs only for meaningful actions.

Do not show unnecessary dialogs.

## Performance

Avoid unnecessary widget rebuilds.

Use pagination/lazy loading for large lists.

Optimize images.

Avoid calling APIs repeatedly during rebuilds.